Then: when do synchronous code, microtasks and tasks run? When does the browser get a chance to render? Why does a long chain of microtasks freeze the page while the same work split across `setTimeout` calls does not?

questions.solution:
```js
function debounce(fn, wait) {
  let timer = null;
  let lastThis, lastArgs;
  function debounced(...args) {
    lastThis = this;
    lastArgs = args;
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => {
      timer = null;
      fn.apply(lastThis, lastArgs);
    }, wait);
  }
  debounced.cancel = () => {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = null;
  };
  return debounced;
}

function throttle(fn, wait) {
  let last = 0;
  let timer = null;
  let pendingThis, pendingArgs;
  return function throttled(...args) {
    const remaining = wait - (Date.now() - last);
    if (remaining <= 0) {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      timer = null;
      last = Date.now();
      fn.apply(this, args);
    } else {
      pendingThis = this;
      pendingArgs = args;
      timer ??= setTimeout(() => {
        timer = null;
        last = Date.now();
        fn.apply(pendingThis, pendingArgs);
      }, remaining);
    }
  };
}
```

Search box: debounce, so the request goes out once the user pauses. Resize or scroll position: throttle (or `requestAnimationFrame`) so the UI keeps updating during the gesture. Infinite scroll: an IntersectionObserver beats either. Double submit: disable the button while the request is in flight; a leading debounce also works.

question:
Implement `runWithLimit(tasks, limit)`:

- `tasks` is an array of functions, each returning a promise when called (a network request, say);
- at most `limit` tasks run at any moment, and the next one starts as soon as one finishes;
- it returns a promise of the results, in the same order as `tasks`;
- if any task fails, the returned promise rejects with that error.

Say what your version does when `tasks` is empty and when `limit` is larger than the number of tasks.

questions.solution:
```js
async function runWithLimit(tasks, limit) {
  const results = new Array(tasks.length);
  let next = 0;
  async function worker() {
    while (next < tasks.length) {
      const i = next++;
      results[i] = await tasks[i]();
    }
  }
  const workers = Array.from({ length: Math.min(limit, tasks.length) }, worker);
  await Promise.all(workers);
  return results;
}
```

`next++` is safe without locks: JavaScript runs one callback at a time, and each worker reads and bumps `next` synchronously. `Promise.all` over the workers rejects with the first error; the other workers keep running their current task, and a `failed` flag checked in the loop stops them from taking new ones. Batching in fixed groups of `limit` (`await Promise.all(batch)`) is a weaker answer: one slow task holds up its whole batch.

```js
function promiseAll(iterable) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const items = Array.from(iterable);
    const results = new Array(items.length);
    let pending = items.length;
    if (pending === 0) return resolve(results);
    items.forEach((item, i) => {
      Promise.resolve(item).then((value) => {
        results[i] = value;
        if (--pending === 0) resolve(results);
      }, reject);
    });
  });
}

function promiseAllSettled(iterable) {
  return promiseAll(
    Array.from(iterable, (item) =>
      Promise.resolve(item).then(
        (value) => ({ status: 'fulfilled', value }),
        (reason) => ({ status: 'rejected', reason }),
      ),
    ),
  );
}
```

`Array.from` accepts any iterable (a `Set`, a generator). A promise settles once, so later `reject` calls after the first are ignored.

1. `user.hi()` → `this === user` → `'Ada'`.
2. `f()` → plain call; strict mode gives `this === undefined` → `undefined`.
3. The `function` passed to `setTimeout` is not called as a method of `user`, so `this` is not `user`: browsers call it with `window` (it logs `window.name`, normally an empty string), Node with a Timeout object (it logs `undefined`). An arrow function or `.bind(this)` fixes it.
4. Arrows have no `this` of their own; at the top of a module it is `undefined` → `undefined`.

```js
function myBind(fn, thisArg, ...preset) {
  return function bound(...args) {
    if (new.target) return new fn(...preset, ...args);
    return fn.apply(thisArg, [...preset, ...args]);
  };
}
```

Under `new`, the native `bind` ignores `thisArg` and constructs the target with the preset arguments; `new fn(...)` does the same. (A detail: with the native version, `obj instanceof bound` checks `fn.prototype`; this sketch returns an instance of `fn`, so `instanceof fn` holds.)

question:
Implement an `Emitter` class in the code box:

- `on(type, handler)`: subscribes and returns a function that unsubscribes;
- `off(type, handler)`: unsubscribes;
- `once(type, handler)`: runs at most once;
- `emit(type, ...args)`: calls the event's handlers in subscription order.

When a handler calls `off` during an `emit` (or a `once` handler removes itself), that `emit` must not skip or repeat any other handler.

questions.solution:
```js
class Emitter {
  #handlers = new Map();

  on(type, handler) {
    if (!this.#handlers.has(type)) this.#handlers.set(type, []);
    this.#handlers.get(type).push(handler);
    return () => this.off(type, handler);
  }

  off(type, handler) {
    const list = this.#handlers.get(type);
    if (!list) return;
    const i = list.indexOf(handler);
    if (i !== -1) list.splice(i, 1);
    if (!list.length) this.#handlers.delete(type);
  }

  once(type, handler) {
    const off = this.on(type, (...args) => {
      off();
      handler(...args);
    });
    return off;
  }

  emit(type, ...args) {
    // A copy: handlers removed during this emit don't shift the loop.
    for (const handler of [...(this.#handlers.get(type) ?? [])]) handler(...args);
  }
}
```

With the copy, a handler removed mid-emit still runs in this emit, which is what Node's `EventEmitter` does; DOM `EventTarget` instead skips listeners removed during dispatch (it flags them removed). Either is fine if the candidate states the choice. To let `off(type, original)` cancel a `once`, store the original on the wrapper (`wrapper.original = handler`) and compare against both in `off`.

